# Line endings that matter.
#
# The Windows installer ships shell scripts THROUGH a Windows checkout and runs
# them inside WSL. A CRLF provision.sh fails on its own shebang line with
# "bad interpreter: No such file or directory" — a baffling error for a file that
# is plainly there. Pinning LF here is the fix; windows/installer/lanex.iss also
# strips CRs at run time as a second line of defence.
*.sh          text eol=lf
*.bash        text eol=lf

# Go's formatter emits LF, full stop — there is no CRLF-flavoured gofmt. Without
# this pin a Windows checkout gets CRLF .go files and `gofmt -l` lists every one
# of them, so the CI gofmt gate on windows-latest fails 100% of the time and
# says only "gofmt would change: main.go probe.go ..." — which reads like a
# formatting problem in code that is perfectly formatted.
*.go          text eol=lf
go.mod        text eol=lf
go.sum        text eol=lf

# Windows-native tooling expects CRLF (and .bat in particular can misbehave
# without it).
*.bat         text eol=crlf
*.cmd         text eol=crlf
*.iss         text eol=crlf
*.manifest    text eol=crlf

# Never touched by line-ending or encoding conversion.
*.ico         binary
*.png         binary
*.gds         binary
*.vcd         binary
